---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Levels from '../components/Levels.astro';
import Genealogy from '../components/Genealogy.astro';
import Garden from '../components/Garden.astro';
import { strands, talks, thesis } from '../data/research';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const selected = strands.flatMap((s) => s.papers.filter((p) => p.selected));
const posts = (await getCollection('writing', ({ data }) => !data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 5);
---
<Base current="home">
  <div class="spread section hero">
    <div class="main">
      <h1>Christian Weilbach</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I study how learning systems can improve themselves and stay correct. I am most interested in the
        organisations around them, since the structures that check a learner have to learn and grow together with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I did my PhD in machine learning at the <a href={thesis.url}>University of British Columbia</a> with
        <a href={thesis.advisor.url}>Frank Wood</a>, on how the structure of a problem can be translated into the
        neural networks that solve it. Before that I wrote my Bachelor's and Master's theses at Heidelberg University, on online
        learning in spiking neuron models and on dictionary learning with Bayesian GANs, and studied philosophy and
        cultural anthropology. I am now the CEO of <a href="https://simm.is">Simmis</a>, where we build software for
        organisations of people and AI agents. The open-source stack underneath it covers versioned memory, forkable
        runtimes, probabilistic inference and a language whose kernel checks its own optimisations.
        <a href="/about">More about me</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="aside" id="contact">
      <img class="portrait" src="/christian_squamish.png" alt="Christian Weilbach outdoors in Squamish, British Columbia" width="160" height="160" />
      <div class="contact">
        <span>christian@weilbach.name</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/whilo">GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=6foQfZwAAAAJ&hl=en">Google Scholar</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/christian-weilbach-1bab251b0/">LinkedIn</a>
        <a href="/cv">CV</a>
        <a href="https://cal.com/christian-weilbach-k3cf0p/30min">Book a 30-minute call</a>
      </div>
      <Garden />
    </aside>
  </div>

  <section class="section grown">
    <div class="spread">
      <div class="main">
        <h2>Projects and papers since 2006</h2>
        <p>The figure shows what I have worked on and which project grew out of which. Some branches ended, and a few came back years later. Scroll to replay the years.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Genealogy />
  </section>

  <div class="spread section">
    <div class="main">
      <h2>How the projects relate</h2>
      <p>
        My projects form a stack, every level supports the same operation. A person, an agent or a program
        makes an attempt on a fork and compares the result with evidence. The attempt is adopted only after a check.
        All levels share one versioned memory, so forking is cheap.
      </p>
      <Levels />
    </div>
    <aside class="aside margin-note">
      <p>A system can run this loop well and still pursue the wrong goal. So each level also carries a question about its purpose, which appears when you open the level.</p>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <div class="spread section">
    <div class="main">
      <h2>Current work</h2>
      <ul class="entries">
        <li>
          <a class="title" href="https://github.com/replikativ/klara">Klara</a>
          <span class="meta">A language in which a running program keeps earlier versions of itself and tries optimisations on a fork. A small kernel checks each optimisation before the program adopts it. Research prototype.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="title" href="https://github.com/replikativ/foerster">Foerster</a>
          <span class="meta">Probabilistic inference that forks a running simulation instead of re-running it. A <a href="https://simm.is/blog/stuttgart-in-a-posterior">simulation of Stuttgart</a> with 936,597 people is built on it. Beta.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="title" href="https://datahike.io">Datahike</a>
          <span class="meta">A Datalog database that keeps every past state queryable. A branch shares all unchanged storage. Stratum, Proximum and Scriptum apply the same model to columnar SQL, vector search and full text. Datahike is used in production and close to 1.0.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="title" href="https://github.com/replikativ/raster">Raster</a>
          <span class="meta">Numerical computing for the JVM that compiles the same code for different devices. It runs the Stuttgart simulation, and a LeNet-5 training step on CPU ran 1.7 to 2.4 times faster than JAX in our measurements. Beta on the JVM, alpha on GPUs.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="title" href="https://simm.is">Simmis</a>
          <span class="meta">A workspace in which people and AI agents share one memory. Work happens on branches and joins the shared state after review. Early access.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I am looking for people who want to work on self-improving systems, in programming languages, probabilistic
        inference or systems engineering. If that is you, write to me at christian@weilbach.name or
        <a href="https://cal.com/christian-weilbach-k3cf0p/30min">book a 30-minute call</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {posts.length > 0 && (
    <div class="spread section">
      <div class="main">
        <h2>Writing</h2>
        <ul class="entries">
          {posts.map((p) => (
            <li><a class="title" href={`/writing/${p.id}/`}>{p.data.title}</a><span class="meta">{p.data.description}</span></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  )}

  <div class="spread section">
    <div class="main">
      <h2>Selected papers</h2>
      <ul class="entries">
        {selected.map((p) => (
          <li>
            {p.url ? <a class="title" href={p.url}>{p.title}</a> : <span class="title">{p.title}</span>}
            <span class="meta">{p.authors}. <span class="venue">{p.venue}</span>{p.year && `, ${p.year}`}.{p.note && <span class="note"> {p.note[0].toUpperCase() + p.note.slice(1)}.</span>}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="small"><a href="/research">All research, with figures and code</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="spread section">
    <div class="main">
      <h2>Talks</h2>
      <ul class="entries">
        {talks.map((t) => (
          <li>
            {t.url ? <a class="title" href={t.url}>{t.title}</a> : <span>{t.title}</span>}
            <span class="meta">{t.where}{t.date && `, ${t.date}`}.</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { padding-top: clamp(40px, 9vh, 96px); }
  .hero .main { gap: 26px; }
  .grown .main { gap: 14px; }
  .margin-note { padding-top: 84px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; }
  @media (max-width: 980px) { .margin-note { order: 2; padding-top: 0; } }
  .portrait { width: 160px; height: 160px; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 50%; }
  .contact { display: grid; gap: 4px; }
  .contact span { color: var(--ink); user-select: all; }
  @media (max-width: 980px) {
    .hero .aside { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 18px; }
    .portrait { width: 88px; height: 88px; }
    .contact { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 4px 16px; }
  }
</style>
